## VERDICT ON THE CRITICAL QUESTION

**The demos are 100% RECREATED — hand-written lookalike markup. Zero real app components. Zero React. Zero animation library.** The deck is a single standalone `.html` file with inline `<style>` + inline `<script>`, no build step, no imports, no `<script src>`. Proof: `grep -ci "gsap\|framer\|motion\.\|<script src" announcement-preview.html` → **0**; `grep -c requestAnimationFrame` → **0**. `framer-motion@12.34.0` IS in package.json and IS used elsewhere in `src/` (e.g. `src/components/dashboard/DashboardKPICards.tsx`, `src/components/meta/CreateCampaignWizard.tsx`) but is **never** touched by the deck. No `gsap` dependency exists at all.

## 1. FILE TOPOLOGY

Two near-identical twins (388 KB, 4025 lines):
- `/Users/ismailelqorchi/Desktop/iGrowFast/New Website/iGrowFast/announcement-preview.html` — local review copy, asset paths `public/Logo/…`, `public/BG.svg`.
- `/Users/ismailelqorchi/Desktop/iGrowFast/New Website/iGrowFast/public/announcement-deck.html` — the shipped one (4031 lines). Only diffs: paths rewritten `public/X` → `/X`, plus a 6-line app-embed override block injected at line 1109:
```html
<style>
  /* App-embed overrides: transparent backdrop over <AuthBackground/> */
  html, body { background: transparent !important; }
  .veil { background: transparent !important; background-image: none !important; backdrop-filter: none !important; }
  .modal { background: rgba(18,18,17,0.82) !important; }
</style>
```
**There is NO "slides pipeline."** `AnnouncementDeck.tsx:15` claims the deck is "built by the slides pipeline" — this is stale/false. No `slides*` dir, no `scripts/`, no announcement entry in `package.json`. The file is hand-maintained and the two copies are manually kept in sync. Anything reusing this technique inherits that: **the HTML is the source, written by hand.**

## 2. STAGE SIZING — THE CORE TRICK

Every demo is a **fixed design canvas** (`.demo-stage`, hardcoded px) uniformly scaled into a fluid frame (`.demo-frame`) via `transform: scale()` with `transform-origin: top left`.

```css
.demo-stage {
  position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0;
  transform-origin: top left;
  user-select: none;
}
```
Markup carries the canvas dims on the frame, and the stage repeats them as literal px (`announcement-preview.html:1199-1200`):
```html
<div class="demo-frame" data-demo="1" data-w="960" data-h="620">
  <div class="demo-stage wd-root" style="width:960px;height:620px;display:grid;grid-template-columns:360px minmax(0,1fr)">
```
The fit loop (`announcement-preview.html:2890-2899`) — **VERBATIM**:
```js
// fit all demo stages
const fit = (frame) => {
  const stage = frame.querySelector('.demo-stage');
  if (!stage) return;
  const w = parseInt(frame.dataset.w, 10), h = parseInt(frame.dataset.h, 10);
  frame.style.aspectRatio = w + ' / ' + h;
  const s = frame.clientWidth / w;
  stage.style.transform = 'scale(' + s + ')';
};
const ro = new ResizeObserver((entries) => entries.forEach((en) => fit(en.target)));
document.querySelectorAll('.demo-frame').forEach((f) => { fit(f); ro.observe(f); });
```
`aspectRatio` is set from the data attrs so the frame reserves correct height; `ResizeObserver` re-fits on any container resize. **This is already fully responsive** — the stage never reflows, it only scales. That is the whole reason the fake UI is pixel-stable.

## 3. THE TIMELINE MECHANISM — async/await + Promise-wrapped setTimeout, NOT rAF/CSS-keyframes

There is no scheduler, no keyframe track, no library. A scene is a **plain `async function` that awaits a sleep between imperative DOM mutations**. Motion itself is delegated to **CSS transitions**, triggered by class toggles; JS only decides *when* to toggle and how long to wait. Counts across the file: `await e.sleep` ×166, `await e.move` ×87, `await e.click` ×62, raw `setTimeout` ×4.

Looping/abort/replay — `startDemo` (`announcement-preview.html:2806-2833`), **VERBATIM**:
```js
function stopDemo() { if (ctl) { ctl.aborted = true; ctl = null; } }

function startDemo(i) {
  stopDemo();
  const pane = host.querySelector('[data-step="' + i + '"]');
  if (!pane) return;
  const frame = pane.querySelector('.demo-frame');
  const stage = pane.querySelector('.demo-stage');
  if (!stage || !frame) return;
  const d = window.DEMOS[parseInt(frame.dataset.demo, 10)];
  if (!d) return;
  if (REDUCED) { try { d.init && d.init(stage); d.staticState && d.staticState(stage); } catch (e) { console.error(e); } return; }
  const myCtl = { aborted: false };
  ctl = myCtl;
  (async () => {
    const e = makeEngine(stage, myCtl);
    try { await e.sleep(400); } catch (_) { return; }
    for (;;) {
      if (myCtl.aborted) return;
      try {
        d.init && d.init(stage);
        await d.scene(e, stage);
      } catch (err) {
        if (err === ABORT || myCtl.aborted) return;
        console.error('[demo ' + i + ']', err);
        try { await e.sleep(1500); } catch (_) { return; }
      }
    }
  })();
}
```
Key design: **`for(;;)` infinite replay**; each iteration calls `d.init(stage)` to hard-reset all DOM to frame zero, then runs `d.scene()` once. Cancellation is a **token + rejected promise**: `const ABORT = Symbol('abort')` (line 2781); every `sleep` rejects with `ABORT` when `ctl.aborted`, which unwinds the entire async scene stack instantly at the next await. Leaving a slide sets `aborted = true` → scene dies mid-flight. Only ONE demo runs at a time (`stopDemo()` first). `REDUCED` (`prefers-reduced-motion`) short-circuits to a static end-state via `d.staticState(stage)`.

## 4. THE ENGINE — every reusable primitive, VERBATIM

`makeEngine` (`announcement-preview.html:2917-2966`) — this is the entire reusable kit:
```js
function makeEngine(stage, ctl) {
  // ensure cursor + park it
  let cursor = stage.querySelector(':scope > .wd-cursor');
  if (!cursor) {
    cursor = document.createElement('div');
    cursor.className = 'wd-cursor';
    cursor.innerHTML = '<svg class="cursor-scale" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M5.5 3.2v16.9c0 .45.55.68.87.36l4.1-4.1c.1-.1.23-.15.36-.15h5.85c.46 0 .68-.55.36-.87L6.37 2.85c-.32-.32-.87-.1-.87.35Z" fill="#fff" stroke="#141413" stroke-width="1.3"/></svg>';
    stage.appendChild(cursor);
  }
  const designW = stage.offsetWidth;
  const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((res, rej) => {
    if (ctl.aborted) return rej(ABORT);
    setTimeout(() => (ctl.aborted ? rej(ABORT) : res()), ms);
  });
  const move = async (x, y, ms = 700) => {
    cursor.style.transitionDuration = ms + 'ms';
    cursor.style.transform = 'translate(' + x + 'px,' + y + 'px)';
    await sleep(ms + 40);
  };
  const click = async (x, y, dark = false) => {
    cursor.classList.add('is-down');
    const rip = document.createElement('div');
    rip.className = 'wd-ripple' + (dark ? ' dark' : '');
    rip.style.left = (x + 4) + 'px';
    rip.style.top = (y + 4) + 'px';
    stage.appendChild(rip);
    await sleep(140);
    cursor.classList.remove('is-down');
    setTimeout(() => rip.remove(), 500);
    await sleep(120);
  };
  const center = (elOrSel) => {
    const target = typeof elOrSel === 'string' ? stage.querySelector(elOrSel) : elOrSel;
    const s = stage.getBoundingClientRect().width / designW;
    const r = target.getBoundingClientRect();
    const st = stage.getBoundingClientRect();
    return { x: (r.left - st.left + r.width / 2) / s, y: (r.top - st.top + r.height / 2) / s };
  };
  const typeText = async (spanEl, text, msPerChar = 26) => {
    const clip = spanEl.closest('.wd-input') || spanEl.parentElement;
    const pill = spanEl.closest('.wd-composer-pill');
    for (let i = 0; i < text.length; i++) {
      spanEl.textContent += text[i];
      if (i === 0 && pill) pill.classList.add('has-text');
      clip.scrollLeft = clip.scrollWidth;
      await sleep(text[i] === ' ' ? 13 : msPerChar);
    }
  };
  return { sleep, move, click, center, typeText, cursor, q: (s) => stage.querySelector(s), qa: (s) => [...stage.querySelectorAll(s)] };
}
```
**The `center()` scale-recovery trick is the subtlest and most important part.** `designW = stage.offsetWidth` returns the *layout* width (960) because `offsetWidth` ignores CSS transforms; `getBoundingClientRect().width` returns the *visual* (scaled) width. Dividing recovers the live scale `s`, converting a real on-screen rect back into **design-space coordinates**. That is why every scene can hardcode cursor coords in 960×620 space and still land correctly at any rendered size.

