```
`postMessage(..., '*')` with no `origin` check on the receiving `onMessage` handler (`AnnouncementDeck.tsx:53-57`) — acceptable here (worst case: premature dismiss of a cosmetic overlay), but do not copy that laxity into anything that carries state.

## 9. DIMENSIONS / ASPECT RATIOS / RESPONSIVE PATH

Canvases (from `data-w`/`data-h`), all landscape:
- **960×620** (≈1.548:1) — demos 1, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 14 — the dominant format.
- **960×600** (1.6:1 / 8:5) — demos 2, 3, 4.
- **820×660** (≈1.242:1) — demo 13 (the orders-export wizard, near-square).

So "960×620" from the brief is the modal canvas — accurate for 8 of 12 demos, but **not uniform**; three are 960×600 and one is 820×660. Shell: `.modal { width: min(1200px, 100%); height: min(780px, calc(100vh - 40px)); }`. Demo column sits in a `minmax(330px,5fr) / minmax(0,8fr)` grid (copy left, demo right).

**Responsiveness for a marketing page — mostly already solved, three gaps:**
1. **Scaling: free.** `fit()` + `ResizeObserver` + `aspectRatio` already make any `.demo-frame` fluid at any width. Drop the frame into a fluid container and it just works. Nothing to change.
2. **Legibility floor: the real problem.** At 960 design width, body text is 13–15px. On a 390px phone, `s ≈ 0.40` → ~5.6px text. Unreadable. The deck dodges this entirely — its `@media (max-width: 980px)` block (`:339-346`) never shrinks the stage; it only unstacks the grid and lets the page scroll:
```css
@media (max-width: 980px) {
  body { overflow: auto; padding: 16px; }
  .modal { height: auto; max-height: none; }
  .step-pane { position: relative; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 24px; padding: 22px 20px; display: none; }
  .step-pane.is-active { display: grid; }
  .body { overflow: visible; }
  .segments { width: 120px; }
}
```
For marketing you must add one of: a **portrait/mobile variant canvas** per demo (e.g. 420×720, authored separately — the honest fix), a **crop-and-pan** (scale to legibility, translate the stage to the region in focus, `overflow:hidden`), or **min-scale + horizontal scroll**. There is no free lunch — a fixed canvas cannot reflow.
3. **Autoplay/visibility.** `startDemo` is driven only by slide navigation. On a marketing page, demos must start on scroll-into-view and stop on exit, or N concurrent `for(;;)` loops burn CPU. Wire `IntersectionObserver` → `startDemo`/`stopDemo` (the `{aborted}` token already supports exactly this; it is per-instance, so lift the module-level `let ctl` into a per-frame map to run several demos independently).

Also required to reuse standalone:
- Fonts are **external Google Fonts** (`Inter`, `Poppins`, `Lora`) via `<link>` — the deck is otherwise self-contained. Assets `/Logo/iGrowFast Logo Transparent.png` and `/BG.svg` are runtime-fetched, with a JS `onerror` text fallback for the logo.
- The **global-ID coupling** (§5) blocks two instances of the same demo per page. Refactor `$ = document.getElementById` → `e.q('#…')` (stage-scoped) before duplicating.
- `prefers-reduced-motion` is honored two ways and both must be kept: CSS kills all animation and hides `.wd-cursor` (`:347-350`), and `startDemo` swaps to `staticState()`. **Every new demo owes a `staticState`.**

## 10. HONEST ASSESSMENT FOR REUSE

The technique is sound and the primitives (`fit`/`ResizeObserver` scaling, `center()` scale-recovery, abortable `sleep`, cursor+ripple, `is-*` class vocabulary, `init`/`scene`/`staticState` contract, `.wd-fade` reset) are genuinely reusable and worth extracting to a small module — maybe 80 lines of JS + 40 lines of CSS. That part is high quality and clearly battle-tested (the srcdoc/`replaceState` comment is evidence of real debugging).

The **cost** is what nobody should understate: the fidelity comes entirely from hand-rebuilding each screen in throwaway namespaced CSS + inline styles, then hand-choreographing every beat in imperative DOM mutations. This file is ~4000 lines for 12 demos — roughly **330 lines per demo**, and it drifts from the real app the moment the app changes, with no test or pipeline to catch it. There is no shortcut back to real components: the whole approach depends on the UI being frozen at a fixed pixel canvas. Budget accordingly, and expect the marketing demos to be net-new authored screens, not a port.
